plan.text:
{"t":"scope","verdict":"in","reason":"Slope and intercept of straight lines are core school algebra."}
{"t":"warmup","id":"w1","prompt":"Two lines: line A is higher on the page at x = 0, line B rises faster. Which has the bigger slope?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Line A, because it starts higher","correct":false},{"id":"b","text":"Line B, because it rises faster","correct":true},{"id":"c","text":"They match, because both go upward","correct":false}]}
{"t":"objective","text":"Understand slope as how fast y changes per step in x, separate from how high the line sits."}
{"t":"segment","n":1,"title":"Height is where you stand","goal":"Read height as the y value at a given x, using the y-intercept.","representation":"graph","misconception":"Thinking a higher line must be a steeper line","check":"tap"}
{"t":"segment","n":2,"title":"Slope as change per step","goal":"Measure slope as rise over run by going one step right and counting the change in y.","representation":"table","misconception":"Reading slope as the y value itself, not the change in y","check":"number"}
{"t":"segment","n":3,"title":"Slope as a speed","goal":"Link slope to a steady rate, like money saved per week, versus the starting amount.","representation":"analogy","misconception":"Mixing up the starting amount with the rate of change","check":"choice"}
{"t":"segment","n":4,"title":"Moving m and c","goal":"Use sliders to see m tilt the line and c slide it, without changing tilt.","representation":"graph","misconception":"Believing changing the intercept also changes the steepness","check":"set"}

segment:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Here are two lines; the first, A, has height 4 when x is 0, and B has height 0."}
{"t":"math","id":"s1ea","latex":"A:\\ y = x + 4","expr":"y = x + 4"}
{"t":"math","id":"s1eb","latex":"B:\\ y = 2x","expr":"y = 2*x"}
{"t":"say","text":"Let's draw both on one graph and see where each one starts."}
{"t":"plot","id":"s1p","x":[-1,5],"y":[-1,10],"fns":[{"id":"s1fa","expr":"x + 4","label":"A: y = x + 4"},{"id":"s1fb","expr":"2*x","label":"B: y = 2x"}]}
{"t":"point","id":"s1pa","plot":"s1p","at":[0,4],"label":"A starts at 4","on":"s1fa"}
{"t":"point","id":"s1pb","plot":"s1p","at":[0,0],"label":"B starts at 0","on":"s1fb"}
{"t":"say","text":"Where a line crosses the y-axis is its y-intercept, which is just its height when x is zero."}
{"t":"guide","id":"s1g","plot":"s1p","x":0,"label":"x = 0"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s1pa","note":"y-intercept of A"}
{"t":"say","text":"It's tempting to think the higher line must be steeper, so watch what B does next."}
{"t":"point","id":"s1pm","plot":"s1p","at":[4,8],"label":"B catches A","on":"s1fb"}
{"t":"math","id":"s1m","latex":"4 + 4 = 2 \\times 4 = 8","expr":"4 + 4 = 2*4"}
{"t":"say","text":"B started lower but climbs faster, so at x equals 4 both lines are at height 8."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"s1pm","note":"B overtakes A"}
{"t":"write","id":"s1n","text":"Height = where it starts. Steepness = how fast it climbs.","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now use the graph to read a height, and be careful about which line you are on."}
{"t":"point","id":"s1c1","plot":"s1p","at":[2,6],"on":"s1fa"}
{"t":"point","id":"s1c2","plot":"s1p","at":[3,6],"on":"s1fb"}
{"t":"point","id":"s1c3","plot":"s1p","at":[1,2],"on":"s1fb"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s1q1","text":"So what does steepness measure, then?"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s1q2","text":"Can a line start higher and still end lower?"}
{"t":"check","id":"s1c","kind":"tap","prompt":"Tap the point on line A where its height is 6.","figure":"s1p","answer":"s1c1","explain":"At x = 2, line A has y = 2 + 4 = 6, so that is where A reaches height 6.","hint":"Height means the y value. Check which line each point sits on, too."}

segment:2.text:
{"t":"page","title":"Slope: change per step"}
{"t":"say","text":"Let's measure a line's steepness using a table, taking one step right each time."}
{"t":"math","id":"s2eq","latex":"y = 3x + 2","expr":"y = 3*x + 2"}
{"t":"table","id":"s2tb","head":["x","y"],"rows":[["0","2"],["1","5"],["2","8"],["3","11"]],"fn":"3*x + 2"}
{"t":"say","text":"Watch the y column: each step right of 1 changes y by the same amount."}
{"t":"math","id":"s2d1","latex":"5 - 2 = 3","expr":"5 - 2 = 3"}
{"t":"math","id":"s2d2","latex":"8 - 5 = 3","expr":"8 - 5 = 3"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"s2tb","note":"y goes up 3 each step"}
{"t":"say","text":"That change in y is the slope: rise over run, here 3 up for 1 across."}
{"t":"math","id":"s2m","latex":"\\text{slope} = \\frac{\\text{rise}}{\\text{run}} = \\frac{3}{1} = 3","expr":"3/1 = 3"}
{"t":"say","text":"Tempting mistake: reading the slope from a y value, like 8 at x equals 2."}
{"t":"write","id":"s2w","text":"y = 8 is a height, not a slope","role":"note"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s2w","note":"height ≠ change"}
{"t":"say","text":"A y value says where you are; the slope says how much y changes per step."}
{"t":"say","text":"Now try a new line, y equals 5x minus 1, and count the change."}
{"t":"ask","id":"s2q1","text":"What if the run is 2, not 1?"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s2q2","text":"Can the slope be negative?"}
{"t":"check","id":"s2c","kind":"number","prompt":"For y = 5x − 1, y is −1 at x = 0 and 4 at x = 1. What is the slope?","answer":"5","anticipated":[{"value":"4","misconception":"Read the slope as the y value at x = 1 instead of the change in y"},{"value":"-1","misconception":"Took the starting height (y at x = 0) as the slope"}],"explain":"y rises from −1 to 4, a change of 5 for one step right, so the slope is 5.","hint":"Subtract the first y from the second y."}

segment:3.text:
{"t":"page","title":"Slope as a speed"}
{"t":"say","text":"Think of saving money: you start with some cash, then add the same amount every week."}
{"t":"write","id":"s3w1","text":"Start with £5 in a jar","role":"line"}
{"t":"write","id":"s3w2","text":"Add £2 every week","role":"line"}
{"t":"say","text":"Let x be the weeks passed and y the money in the jar, in pounds."}
{"t":"math","id":"s3e1","latex":"y = 2x + 5","expr":"y = 2*x + 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"The 2 is the speed of saving, the slope. The 5 is only where you started."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s3e1","note":"2 = rate, 5 = start"}
{"t":"say","text":"Check it with a table: each week the jar grows by 2, whatever the start."}
{"t":"table","id":"s3tb","head":["Weeks x","Money y (£)"],"rows":[["0","5"],["1","7"],["2","9"],["3","11"]],"fn":"2*x + 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now a friend starts with £20 but adds only £1 a week."}
{"t":"math","id":"s3e2","latex":"y = 1x + 20","expr":"y = 1*x + 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"Your friend has more money at the start, but your jar grows faster, so you catch up."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s3e2","note":"bigger start, slower rate"}
{"t":"say","text":"So a big starting number does not mean a big slope; slope only measures the growth per week."}
{"t":"say","text":"Let's see if that idea holds up on your own example."}
{"t":"ask","id":"s3q1","text":"Can the slope be negative?"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s3q2","text":"Where is the start on a graph?"}
{"t":"check","id":"s3c","kind":"choice","prompt":"Sam's jar follows y = 3x + 10 (x in weeks, y in pounds). How fast is Sam saving?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"£3 per week","correct":true,"value":"3"},{"id":"b","text":"£10 per week","correct":false,"misconception":"Took the starting amount as the rate of saving","value":"10"},{"id":"c","text":"£13 per week","correct":false,"misconception":"Added the two numbers together to get the speed","value":"13"},{"id":"d","text":"£30 per week","correct":false,"misconception":"Multiplied the two numbers instead of reading each one's job","value":"30"}],"explain":"The number multiplying x is the slope: £3 more each week. The 10 is just the start.","hint":"Which number changes the jar each time a week passes?"}

segment:4.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Every straight line can be written in this form, with two numbers, m and c."}
{"t":"page","title":"Moving m and c"}
{"t":"math","id":"s4eq","latex":"y = mx + c","expr":"y = m*x + c"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here are two lines: the second is the first, lifted up by two."}
{"t":"plot","id":"s4p","x":[-1,4],"y":[-1,7],"fns":[{"id":"s4f1","expr":"x + 1","label":"y = x + 1"},{"id":"s4f2","expr":"x + 3","label":"y = x + 3"}]}
{"t":"point","id":"s4a","plot":"s4p","at":[0,1],"label":"c = 1","on":"s4f1"}
{"t":"point","id":"s4b","plot":"s4p","at":[0,3],"label":"c = 3","on":"s4f2"}
{"t":"say","text":"A tempting idea says the higher start must be steeper, so let's test it with one step right."}
{"t":"line","id":"s4r1","plot":"s4p","from":[1,1],"to":[1,2],"label":"up 1"}
{"t":"line","id":"s4r2","plot":"s4p","from":[1,3],"to":[1,4],"label":"up 1"}
{"t":"say","text":"Both lines rise by exactly one, so lifting the line moved it without tilting it."}
{"t":"math","id":"s4m","latex":"\\text{rise} = 4 - 3 = 1","expr":"4 - 3 = 1"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s4m","note":"same rise, same steepness"}
{"t":"say","text":"So c slides the line up or down, and m alone sets the tilt."}
{"t":"explore","id":"s4x","x":[-4,4],"y":[-6,8],"expr":"m*x + c","params":[{"name":"m","min":-3,"max":3,"start":1},{"name":"c","min":-3,"max":5,"start":1}],"prompt":"Drag c, then m. Which slider changes the tilt?"}
{"t":"say","text":"Play with both sliders, then make the line steeper than it starts."}
{"t":"ask","id":"s4q1","text":"What happens if m is negative?"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s4q2","text":"Can I change both m and c together?"}
{"t":"check","id":"s4c","kind":"set","prompt":"Make the line steeper than y = x + 1, rising more than 1 per step. Set the right slider.","explore":"s4x","param":"m","target":[1.5,3],"explain":"Yes: steepness comes from m alone, and sliding c just moves the line without tilting it.","hint":"Which slider tilts the line? Moving c only slides it up or down."}

reteach:s1c:other.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's try a different line in a table, to separate across from up."}
{"t":"table","id":"r1t","head":["x (across)","y (height)"],"rows":[["0","1"],["1","3"],["2","5"],["3","7"],["4","9"]],"fn":"2*x + 1"}
{"t":"say","text":"Each row is one point: the first number is how far across, the second is how high."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"r1t","note":"across first, height second"}
{"t":"say","text":"So for height 7 on this line, I look down the height column, then read across."}
{"t":"write","id":"r1w","text":"Height 7 → find 7 in the y column → read its x","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"Try that on line A: find where its height is 6, and check you're on line A."}

reteach:s2c:a0.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test your idea that the slope is the height at x equals 1, using two new lines."}
{"t":"plot","id":"r2p","x":[-1,3],"y":[-1,8],"fns":[{"id":"r2fa","expr":"x + 3","label":"line P: y = x + 3"},{"id":"r2fb","expr":"3*x + 1","label":"line Q: y = 3x + 1"}]}
{"t":"point","id":"r2h","plot":"r2p","at":[1,4],"label":"both are 4 high","on":"r2fa"}
{"t":"say","text":"Both lines are 4 high at x equals 1, so your rule would give them the same slope."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r2h","note":"same height here"}
{"t":"say","text":"But the picture disagrees, because line Q climbs much faster than line P."}
{"t":"line","id":"r2la","plot":"r2p","from":[0,3],"to":[1,4],"label":"P: a small climb","dashed":true}
{"t":"line","id":"r2lb","plot":"r2p","from":[0,1],"to":[1,4],"label":"Q: a big climb","dashed":true}
{"t":"say","text":"So slope can't be the height at one spot; it measures how much y climbs between neighbouring steps."}
{"t":"write","id":"r2w","text":"slope = climb in y, not height","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now try the checkpoint again, comparing y at x = 0 with y at x = 1."}

reteach:s2c:a1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test your idea on a different line that also starts at height minus one."}
{"t":"plot","id":"r2p","x":[-1,4],"y":[-4,6],"fns":[{"id":"r2fa","expr":"2*x - 1","label":"y = 2x − 1"},{"id":"r2fb","expr":"-x - 1","label":"a line that falls 1 per step"}]}
{"t":"point","id":"r2p0","plot":"r2p","at":[0,-1],"label":"start: height −1","on":"r2fa"}
{"t":"say","text":"If slope just meant the starting height, minus one, the line would drop as you step right, like this one."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"r2fb","note":"slope −1 would fall"}
{"t":"say","text":"But our line climbs as you step right, so the starting height can't be what slope measures."}
{"t":"point","id":"r2p1","plot":"r2p","at":[1,1],"label":"one step right","on":"r2fa"}
{"t":"line","id":"r2ln","plot":"r2p","from":[1,-1],"to":[1,1],"label":"how far y moved","dashed":true}
{"t":"say","text":"Slope is that vertical move for one step right, not where the line starts, so try the checkpoint again."}

reteach:s2c:other.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test one idea: that the height of a point is the slope."}
{"t":"plot","id":"r2p","x":[-1,4],"y":[-1,10],"fns":[{"id":"r2fa","expr":"2*x + 1","label":"lower line"},{"id":"r2fb","expr":"2*x + 5","label":"higher line"}]}
{"t":"point","id":"r2pa","plot":"r2p","at":[1,3],"label":"height 3","on":"r2fa"}
{"t":"point","id":"r2pb","plot":"r2p","at":[1,7],"label":"height 7","on":"r2fb"}
{"t":"say","text":"These heights differ, yet the lines tilt exactly the same and never meet."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r2pa","note":"height 3"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r2pb","note":"height 7, same tilt"}
{"t":"say","text":"So height can't be the tilt; tilt is the climb when you walk one step right."}
{"t":"line","id":"r2run","plot":"r2p","from":[1,3],"to":[2,3],"label":"one step right","dashed":true}
{"t":"line","id":"r2rise","plot":"r2p","from":[2,3],"to":[2,5],"label":"climb in y"}
{"t":"say","text":"Look at the two heights in the checkpoint, ask how far y climbed between them, and try again."}

reteach:s3c:b.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test your idea: if Sam saved £10 per week, the jar would gain £10 every week."}
{"t":"write","id":"r3w1","text":"If it were £10 per week: £10 → £20 → £30 → £40","role":"line"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now here is what Sam's equation, y = 3x + 10, actually gives week by week."}
{"t":"table","id":"r3tb","head":["Week x","Money y (£)"],"rows":[["0","10"],["1","13"],["2","16"],["3","19"]],"fn":"3*x + 10"}
{"t":"say","text":"Compare how far the money moves between each pair of weeks with the £10 jumps we imagined."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"r3tb","note":"jump between rows"}
{"t":"say","text":"Notice the 10 is what the jar holds at week 0, before any saving happens."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r3tb","note":"week 0 = start"}
{"t":"say","text":"Using the jumps between rows, have another go at the checkpoint."}

reteach:s3c:c.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test your idea on a different jar: it starts with £20 and gains £2 every week."}
{"t":"page","title":"Testing 'add the numbers'"}
{"t":"math","id":"r3e1","latex":"y = 2x + 20","expr":"y = 2*x + 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"If speed meant adding the two numbers, this jar would gain £22 a week, so let's check the real money."}
{"t":"table","id":"r3tb","head":["Week x","Money y (£)"],"rows":[["0","20"],["1","22"],["2","24"],["3","26"]],"fn":"2*x + 20"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"r3tb","note":"does it jump by 22?"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here is the same jar as a graph, with one week's step from week 0 to week 1."}
{"t":"plot","id":"r3p","x":[0,4],"y":[18,30],"fns":[{"id":"r3f","expr":"2*x + 20","label":"y = 2x + 20"}]}
{"t":"point","id":"r3p0","plot":"r3p","at":[0,20],"label":"start","on":"r3f"}
{"t":"point","id":"r3p1","plot":"r3p","at":[1,22],"label":"one week later","on":"r3f"}
{"t":"say","text":"The 20 only tells you where the jar began; it never gets added again each week."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r3p0","note":"start: counted once"}
{"t":"say","text":"So in Sam's equation, decide which number is the start and which one changes each week, then try again."}

reteach:s3c:d.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test multiplying the two numbers on a different jar: y equals 4x plus 7."}
{"t":"page","title":"Testing the multiply idea"}
{"t":"math","id":"r3e1","latex":"y = 4x + 7","expr":"y = 4*x + 7"}
{"t":"say","text":"Multiplying would give 4 times 7, so 28 pounds. Let's fill in the real jar week by week."}
{"t":"table","id":"r3tb","head":["Weeks x","Money y (£)"],"rows":[["0","7"],["1","11"],["2","15"],["3","19"]],"fn":"4*x + 7"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r3tb","note":"Week 1 holds 11, not 28"}
{"t":"say","text":"So multiplying breaks. Look down the money column: what happens between each row, and what is already there at week zero?"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"r3tb","note":"Compare each row with the one above"}
{"t":"say","text":"Each number in the equation has its own job, so read them separately, then try the checkpoint again."}

reteach:s4c:other.text:
{"t":"say","text":"I think the slider that lifts the line got moved, so let's test whether lifting makes a line steeper."}
{"t":"table","id":"r4t1","head":["x","y = x + 1"],"rows":[["0","1"],["1","2"],["2","3"],["3","4"]],"fn":"x + 1"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now lift every y by 3, and look at how much y grows with each step."}
{"t":"table","id":"r4t2","head":["x","y = x + 4"],"rows":[["0","4"],["1","5"],["2","6"],["3","7"]],"fn":"x + 4"}
{"t":"math","id":"r4m1","latex":"5 - 4 = 1 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad 2 - 1 = 1","expr":"5 - 4 = 1"}
{"t":"say","text":"Every number is higher, but each step still adds just 1, so lifting never changed the steepness."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"r4m1","note":"same growth per step"}
{"t":"say","text":"So a steeper line needs the y column to grow by more than 1 each step; ask which slider controls that growth."}
{"t":"say","text":"Have another go at the sliders when you're ready."}

solution:s1c.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's work it through together: line A is y equals x plus 4, and we want its height to be 6."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol1a","latex":"x + 4 = 6","expr":"x + 4 = 6"}
{"t":"say","text":"Subtract 4 from both sides to get x on its own."}
{"t":"step","id":"sol1b","latex":"x = 6 - 4","expr":"x = 6 - 4","why":"subtract 4 from both sides","from":"sol1a"}
{"t":"solutions","id":"sol1s","of":"sol1a","var":"x","values":[2],"latex":"x = 2"}
{"t":"say","text":"So line A is 6 high when x is 2, which is the point (2, 6)."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s1c1","note":"A at height 6"}
{"t":"say","text":"The slip was likely tapping where line B is 6 high, or where x is 6; the height is the y value."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s1c2","note":"this is on line B, not A"}
{"t":"say","text":"This idea of reading height from a line will come back in practice, so you'll get another go."}

solution:s2c.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's work it through together, starting with the rule and the two values we were given."}
{"t":"page","title":"Slope of y = 5x − 1"}
{"t":"math","id":"sol2a","latex":"y = 5x - 1","expr":"y = 5*x - 1"}
{"t":"table","id":"sol2t","head":["x","y"],"rows":[["0","-1"],["1","4"]],"fn":"5*x - 1"}
{"t":"say","text":"Slope is how much y changes in one step right, so we subtract the two y values."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol2b","latex":"\\text{change in } y = 4 - (-1)","expr":"4 - (-1) = 5"}
{"t":"step","id":"sol2c","latex":"4 - (-1) = 4 + 1","expr":"4 - (-1) = 4 + 1","why":"subtracting a negative adds","from":"sol2b"}
{"t":"step","id":"sol2d","latex":"4 + 1 = 5","expr":"4 + 1 = 5","why":"add","from":"sol2c"}
{"t":"say","text":"You picked 4, the height at x equals 1, and then −1, the starting height; neither is a change."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"sol2t","note":"4 and −1 are heights"}
{"t":"say","text":"The step is one unit right, so the slope is the change divided by the run."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol2e","latex":"\\text{slope} = \\frac{5}{1} = 5","expr":"5/1 = 5"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"sol2e","note":"slope = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"So the slope is 5, the same number multiplying x, and we'll practise this idea again soon."}

solution:s3c.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's work it through together, starting with Sam's jar rule."}
{"t":"page","title":"Sam's jar, step by step"}
{"t":"math","id":"sol3e1","latex":"y = 3x + 10","expr":"y = 3*x + 10"}
{"t":"say","text":"To see the speed, compare the jar after 0 weeks and after 1 week."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol3e2","latex":"x = 0:\\ y = 3(0) + 10 = 10","expr":"3*0 + 10 = 10"}
{"t":"math","id":"sol3e3","latex":"x = 1:\\ y = 3(1) + 10 = 13","expr":"3*1 + 10 = 13"}
{"t":"say","text":"Going from £10 to £13 in one week is a change of 3."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol3e4","latex":"13 - 10 = 3","expr":"13 - 10 = 3"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"sol3e4","note":"change in one week"}
{"t":"say","text":"You picked £10 and £13, which are heights of the jar: the start, and the total after one week."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"sol3e2","note":"start, not speed"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"sol3e3","note":"total, not speed"}
{"t":"say","text":"Speed is how much the jar grows each week, and that is the number multiplying x."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"sol3e1","note":"3 = £ per week"}
{"t":"say","text":"So Sam saves £3 per week, and this idea will come back in practice."}

solution:s4c.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's start with the line we need to beat, and find its slope."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol4a","latex":"y = x + 1","expr":"y = x + 1"}
{"t":"step","id":"sol4b","latex":"y = 1x + 1","expr":"y = 1*x + 1","why":"the number in front of x is m, so m = 1","from":"sol4a"}
{"t":"say","text":"Your slip was likely moving c, which slides the line up or down without tilting it."}
{"t":"write","id":"sol4n","text":"c moves the line. m tilts it.","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"To rise more than 1 per step, m has to be bigger than 1, so try m = 2."}
{"t":"table","id":"sol4t","head":["x","y = 2x + 1"],"rows":[["0","1"],["1","3"],["2","5"]],"fn":"2*x + 1"}
{"t":"say","text":"Each step right adds 2 to y, which beats the 1 we started with."}
{"t":"write","id":"sol4w","text":"Answer: set m between 1.5 and 3","role":"line"}
{"t":"say","text":"You'll meet this idea again in the practice questions, so you'll get another go."}

lost:1:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's slow down and start with something simple: a rule that turns a number x into a height y."}
{"t":"page","title":"Height from a rule"}
{"t":"math","id":"l1k1a","latex":"y = x + 4","expr":"y = x + 4"}
{"t":"say","text":"To find the height at any x, just put that x into the rule and add 4."}
{"t":"table","id":"l1k1t","head":["x","y"],"rows":[["0","4"],["1","5"],["2","6"],["3","7"]],"fn":"x + 4"}
{"t":"say","text":"Look at the row where x is 2: the height is 6, so the point is (2, 6)."}
{"t":"math","id":"l1k1b","latex":"2 + 4 = 6","expr":"2 + 4 = 6"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"l1k1b","note":"x = 2 gives height 6"}
{"t":"say","text":"The row where x is 0 is special: that height, 4, is where the line starts on the y-axis."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"l1k1t","note":"x = 0 gives the starting height"}
{"t":"say","text":"So height just means the y value for a chosen x. Ready to go back to the graph?"}

lost:2:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's start small: you save money each week, and we just count how much you gain."}
{"t":"page","title":"Slope: counting the gain"}
{"t":"table","id":"l2k1t","head":["Week","Money (£)"],"rows":[["0","2"],["1","5"],["2","8"],["3","11"]],"fn":"3*x + 2"}
{"t":"say","text":"Going from week 0 to week 1, you went from 2 pounds to 5 pounds."}
{"t":"math","id":"l2k1a","latex":"5 - 2 = 3","expr":"5 - 2 = 3"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"l2k1t","note":"one step right"}
{"t":"say","text":"From week 1 to week 2 you went from 5 to 8, so the gain is the same."}
{"t":"math","id":"l2k1b","latex":"8 - 5 = 3","expr":"8 - 5 = 3"}
{"t":"say","text":"That steady gain per step is the slope: here, 3 pounds for every one week."}
{"t":"math","id":"l2k1c","latex":"\\frac{\\text{gain}}{\\text{steps}} = \\frac{3}{1} = 3","expr":"3/1 = 3"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"l2k1c","note":"the slope"}
{"t":"say","text":"So to find a slope, take one step right and subtract; ready to try your question again?"}

lost:3:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's slow down and picture one jar with just three numbers."}
{"t":"page","title":"A jar, week by week"}
{"t":"write","id":"l3k1a","text":"Jar starts with £10","role":"line"}
{"t":"write","id":"l3k1b","text":"Each week we add £3","role":"line"}
{"t":"say","text":"Count the money after each week, and watch what happens from one row to the next."}
{"t":"table","id":"l3k1t","head":["Week","Money (£)"],"rows":[["0","10"],["1","13"],["2","16"],["3","19"]],"fn":"3*x + 10"}
{"t":"say","text":"Every week the money goes up by the same amount, three pounds, no matter which week it is."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"l3k1t","note":"+3 each week"}
{"t":"say","text":"That steady jump is the slope, and the £10 we began with is just the starting amount."}
{"t":"math","id":"l3k1m","latex":"y = 3x + 10","expr":"y = 3*x + 10"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"l3k1m","note":"3 = jump per week, 10 = start"}
{"t":"say","text":"So the number beside x is the speed, and the lone number is where you began; back to the lesson."}

lost:4:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's start small with one rule: y equals 2x plus 1."}
{"t":"page","title":"m and c with numbers"}
{"t":"math","id":"l4k1eq","latex":"y = 2x + 1","expr":"y = 2*x + 1"}
{"t":"say","text":"Try x as 0, 1 and 2, and watch what y does each time."}
{"t":"table","id":"l4k1t1","head":["x","y = 2x + 1"],"rows":[["0","1"],["1","3"],["2","5"]],"fn":"2*x + 1"}
{"t":"say","text":"Every step right adds 2 to y, and that 2 is m, the tilt."}
{"t":"math","id":"l4k1d","latex":"3 - 1 = 2","expr":"3 - 1 = 2"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"l4k1d","note":"m = 2 per step"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now change only the plus 1 to plus 4, and see what moves."}
{"t":"table","id":"l4k1t2","head":["x","y = 2x + 4"],"rows":[["0","4"],["1","6"],["2","8"]],"fn":"2*x + 4"}
{"t":"math","id":"l4k1e","latex":"6 - 4 = 2","expr":"6 - 4 = 2"}
{"t":"say","text":"Every y rose by 3, yet each step still adds 2, so c moved the start and m stayed put."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"l4k1e","note":"same climb, new start"}
{"t":"say","text":"So m is the climb per step and c is the starting height; shall we try the sliders again?"}

answer:s1q1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Steepness measures how much y changes each time x moves one step to the right."}
{"t":"point","id":"a1pb","plot":"s1p","at":[2,4],"label":"(2, 4)","on":"s1fb"}
{"t":"line","id":"a1rb","plot":"s1p","from":[1,2],"to":[2,2],"label":"1 step right","dashed":true}
{"t":"line","id":"a1ub","plot":"s1p","from":[2,2],"to":[2,4],"label":"up 2","dashed":true}
{"t":"say","text":"On line B, one step right takes you from height 2 up to height 4."}
{"t":"math","id":"a1m","latex":"\\frac{4-2}{2-1} = 2","expr":"(4-2)/(2-1) = 2"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"a1m","note":"change in y per 1 step"}
{"t":"say","text":"Line A takes the same step but climbs less, even though it starts higher."}
{"t":"point","id":"a1pa","plot":"s1p","at":[1,5],"label":"(1, 5)","on":"s1fa"}
{"t":"line","id":"a1ra","plot":"s1p","from":[1,5],"to":[2,5],"label":"1 step right","dashed":true}
{"t":"line","id":"a1ua","plot":"s1p","from":[2,5],"to":[2,6],"label":"up 1","dashed":true}
{"t":"say","text":"So B is steeper because its climb per step is bigger, while A just starts higher."}
{"t":"say","text":"Does that fit? Tell me how much line A climbs for each step right."}

answer:s1q2.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Yes, and our board already shows it: line A starts higher, at 4, but line B climbs faster."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s1pa","note":"A starts higher"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s1pb","note":"B starts lower"}
{"t":"say","text":"At x equals 4 they meet, at height 8, so B has caught up."}
{"t":"point","id":"a2m","plot":"s1p","at":[4,8],"label":"same height","on":"s1fb"}
{"t":"say","text":"After that, at x equals 6, B is at 12 but A is only at 10."}
{"t":"point","id":"a2b","plot":"s1p","at":[6,12],"label":"B: 12","on":"s1fb"}
{"t":"point","id":"a2a","plot":"s1p","at":[6,10],"label":"A: 10","on":"s1fa"}
{"t":"say","text":"So the line that started higher ends up lower, because B gains 2 per step and A gains only 1."}
{"t":"line","id":"a2l","plot":"s1p","from":[6,10],"to":[6,12],"label":"B is 2 higher","dashed":true}
{"t":"say","text":"Now you tell me: what would happen at x equals 10, which line is higher there?"}

answer:s2q1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"With a run of 2, jump two steps right: from x equals 0 to x equals 2 in our table."}
{"t":"math","id":"a3r","latex":"\\text{rise} = 8 - 2 = 6","expr":"8 - 2 = 6"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"s2tb","note":"y goes 2 to 8 over two steps"}
{"t":"say","text":"The rise is 6, but it took two steps, so divide the rise by the run."}
{"t":"math","id":"a3s","latex":"\\frac{6}{2} = 3","expr":"6/2 = 3"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"a3s","note":"same slope as before"}
{"t":"say","text":"Same slope as before, because two steps of 3 each make 6, so dividing by the run keeps it fair."}
{"t":"math","id":"a3t","latex":"3 + 3 = 6","expr":"3 + 3 = 6"}
{"t":"say","text":"Try this: if y rises 12 over a run of 4, what is the slope?"}

answer:s2q2.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Yes, slope is negative whenever y goes down as you step right."}
{"t":"math","id":"a4e","latex":"y = 8 - 3x","expr":"y = 8 - 3*x"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here x = 1 gives 5 and x = 2 gives 2, so y drops by 3 each step."}
{"t":"math","id":"a4d","latex":"2 - 5 = -3","expr":"2 - 5 = -3"}
{"t":"say","text":"It's the same rise over run, but the rise is negative."}
{"t":"math","id":"a4m","latex":"\\frac{-3}{1} = -3","expr":"-3/1 = -3"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"a4m","note":"negative slope: y falls"}
{"t":"say","text":"On our graph, a line leaving A's start at 4 and falling to 0 slopes downhill."}
{"t":"line","id":"a4l","plot":"s1p","from":[0,4],"to":[4,0],"label":"falls 1 per step","dashed":true}
{"t":"say","text":"Does that make sense? If y goes 10, then 7, then 4 as x steps up by one, what's the slope?"}

answer:s3q1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Yes it can: a negative slope means the line goes down as you step right."}
{"t":"write","id":"a5w1","text":"Negative slope = money going down","role":"note"}
{"t":"math","id":"a5e1","latex":"y = -3x + 20","expr":"y = -3*x + 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"Think of a jar with £20 where you spend £3 every week."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"a5e1","note":"−3 = spending £3 per week"}
{"t":"say","text":"After one week you have £17, after two weeks £14, so each step right drops y by 3."}
{"t":"math","id":"a5e2","latex":"20 - 3 = 17,\\quad 17 - 3 = 14","expr":"20 - 3 = 17"}
{"t":"say","text":"So rise over run is minus 3 over 1, and the line slopes downhill."}
{"t":"say","text":"Your turn: if a line goes down 4 each time x goes up 1, what is its slope?"}

answer:s3q2.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Good question: the start is where the line crosses the y-axis, which is where x equals zero."}
{"t":"guide","id":"a6g","plot":"s1p","x":0,"label":"x = 0, the start"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s1pa","note":"A starts at 4"}
{"t":"say","text":"In the jar, the start is the £5 you hold before any weeks have passed."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s3w1","note":"start: 0 weeks"}
{"t":"say","text":"In the equation, the start is the number added on at the end."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"s3e1","note":"+ 5 is the start"}
{"t":"say","text":"Line B starts at 0, so it meets the y-axis at the origin."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s1pb","note":"B starts at 0"}
{"t":"say","text":"For y = 1x + 20, where would the start be on the graph?"}

answer:s4q1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"A negative m means each step right takes y down instead of up."}
{"t":"math","id":"a7m","latex":"y = -x + 1","expr":"y = -x + 1"}
{"t":"say","text":"Start at c equals 1, then step one right: y drops from 1 to 0."}
{"t":"math","id":"a7s","latex":"y = -(1) + 1 = 0","expr":"-(1) + 1 = 0"}
{"t":"say","text":"Compare with the board: the blue line rises one per step, but here the rise flips downward."}
{"t":"line","id":"a7d","plot":"s4p","from":[1,2],"to":[1,1],"label":"down 1 when m = −1","dashed":true}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"s4r1","note":"up 1 becomes down 1"}
{"t":"say","text":"The start point is set by c, so the line still crosses the y-axis at 1, only tilted downhill."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s4a","note":"c doesn't change"}
{"t":"say","text":"Drag the m slider below zero to watch it tilt; if m is negative two, what does y do each step?"}

answer:s4q2.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Yes, you can change both, because m and c do two separate jobs."}
{"t":"write","id":"a8w1","text":"m = tilt, c = start height","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"On our board, c moved the line from 1 up to 3, but the rise stayed 1."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"s4b","note":"c moved the start"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"s4r2","note":"rise unchanged"}
{"t":"say","text":"So changing both just tilts the line and slides it, and neither change cancels the other."}
{"t":"math","id":"a8e1","latex":"y = 2x + 3","expr":"y = 2*x + 3"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here m is 2, so each step right goes up 2, and c is 3, so it starts at 3."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"a8e1","note":"m = 2, c = 3"}
{"t":"say","text":"Try it on the sliders: if m is 2 and c is 3, where does the line cross the y-axis?"}

recap.text:
{"t":"idea","id":"i1","text":"The height of a line at any x is its y value; c is the height where it crosses the y-axis.","seg":1}
{"t":"idea","id":"i2","text":"Slope is how much y changes for one step right: rise over run.","seg":2}
{"t":"idea","id":"i3","text":"Slope is a steady rate, like pounds saved per week, while c is the amount you start with.","seg":3}
{"t":"idea","id":"i4","text":"Changing m tilts the line; changing c slides it up or down without changing its tilt.","seg":4}
{"t":"say","text":"You picked line B because it rises faster; does starting higher change which line has the bigger slope?"}
{"t":"check","id":"rp1","kind":"number","idea":"i2","prompt":"A straight line passes through (1, 4) and (2, 10). What is its slope?","answer":"6","anticipated":[{"value":"4","misconception":"Read the height at x = 1 instead of how much y changes per step"},{"value":"10","misconception":"Used the y value at x = 2 rather than the change in y"}],"explain":"Going one step right takes y from 4 to 10, a rise of 6, so the slope is 6.","hint":"Count how much y changes when x goes up by one."}
{"t":"check","id":"rp2","kind":"choice","idea":"i3","prompt":"A plant is 12 cm tall and grows 3 cm each week, so its height is y = 3x + 12 after x weeks. What does the 12 tell us?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Its height at the start, when x = 0","correct":true},{"id":"b","text":"How many cm it grows each week","correct":false,"misconception":"Mixed up the starting amount with the steady rate of change"},{"id":"c","text":"Its height after one week","correct":false,"misconception":"Thought c is the value at x = 1 rather than at x = 0"},{"id":"d","text":"The number of weeks it grows for","correct":false,"misconception":"Treated a number in the rule as a count of weeks instead of a height"}],"explain":"At x = 0 the 3x part is zero, so 12 is the starting height.","hint":"Put x = 0 into the rule and see what is left."}
{"t":"check","id":"rp3","kind":"expr","idea":"i4","prompt":"The line y = 2x + 1 slides up 4 units with no change in tilt. Write its new rule as y = …","answer":"2*x + 5","variable":"x","anticipated":[{"value":"6*x + 1","misconception":"Added 4 to the slope, which tilts the line instead of sliding it"},{"value":"2*x + 4","misconception":"Replaced c with 4 instead of adding 4 to the old c"}],"explain":"Sliding up changes only c, from 1 to 5, and the slope stays 2.","hint":"Which number controls the height at x = 0, and which controls the tilt?"}
